      <h2>Why Do Insurance Companies Look at Credit Scores?</h2>
      <p>
        Insurers aren't checking your credit to guess whether you'll pay your monthly bill on time. They use it because decades of loss data, including major studies by the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the University of Texas, show a strong link between credit stability and claim costs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Across millions of policies, drivers who keep their credit accounts current tend to file fewer claims, submit smaller repair bills, and cost insurers less over time.
      </p>

      <h2>Credit-Based Insurance Score (CBIS) vs. Traditional FICO Score</h2>
      <p>
        Your insurance score isn't the same number a mortgage lender sees. Both pull raw history from Equifax, Experian, or TransUnion, but the weighting is tailored to claim risk instead of loan default risk:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">What Goes into a Credit-Based Insurance Score:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Payment History (~40%):</strong> On-time payments across credit cards and loans over several years.</li>
          <li><strong>Outstanding Debt & Credit Utilization (~30%):</strong> How much of your available revolving credit limit you're currently using.</li>
          <li><strong>Credit History Length (~15%):</strong> How long your oldest account has been open, plus the average age of your accounts.</li>
          <li><strong>Pursuit of New Credit (~10%):</strong> Recent hard pulls from applying for new cards or loans.</li>
          <li><strong>Credit Mix (~5%):</strong> Holding a healthy mix of revolving credit cards and installment loans.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--color-text-muted);">
          <strong>Off-Limits Under Federal Law:</strong> Race, religion, nationality, marital status, gender, ZIP code, salary, bank balances, and investment accounts cannot be used in an insurance score.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The 4 States That Ban or Restrict Credit Scoring</h2>
      <p>
        Lawmakers and consumer advocates in several states have pushed back against credit-based pricing, arguing it hits lower-income drivers hardest. Four states now ban or tightly restrict it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>California:</strong> Proposition 103 requires auto rates to hinge primarily on driving record, annual mileage, and years behind the wheel. Credit scoring is flatly illegal.</li>
        <li><strong>Massachusetts:</strong> State rules prohibit insurers from using credit data to price personal auto policies.</li>
        <li><strong>Hawaii:</strong> State law bars credit scoring in auto underwriting.</li>
        <li><strong>Michigan:</strong> Michigan's 2020 auto reform law banned insurers from using credit scores, education levels, or ZIP codes as direct rating factors.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4 Steps to Lower Your Insurance Costs If You Have Poor Credit</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Quote Carriers With Lighter Credit Penalties:</strong> Every insurer weights credit differently. GEICO and Nationwide tend to add smaller surcharges for low credit than State Farm or Allstate.</li>
        <li><strong>Push Credit Utilization Under 30%:</strong> Paying down high credit card balances moves your insurance score faster than almost anything else.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask for a Re-Score at Renewal:</strong> If your credit climbed over the last six months, call your agent and ask for a "policy re-score." Carriers rarely refresh your score mid-term unless you ask.</li>
        <li><strong>Use Telematics to Prove Your Driving:</strong> When your credit lags behind your driving habits, a telematics app (DriveSafe&Save, Snapshot, Drivewise) lets your actual braking, speed, and mileage earn you a lower rate.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Your 90-Day Action Plan Before Shopping for Renewal Quotes</h2>
      <p>
        A speeding ticket sticks to your driving record for three to five years. Credit improvements work much faster—if you time things right, they can lower your bill at your very next 6-month renewal:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Days 1–15 (Clean Up Reporting Errors):</strong> Pull your free credit reports at <a href="https://www.annualcreditreport.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AnnualCreditReport.com</a> and check your LexisNexis C.L.U.E. file. Dispute paid medical collections or late payments that don't belong there.</li>
        <li><strong>Days 16–45 (Get Balances Under 20%):</strong> Pay credit card balances down before the statement closing date, not just the due date. That's the balance snapshot card issuers report to the bureaus each month.</li>
        <li><strong>Days 46–75 (Hold Off on New Credit):</strong> Skip store cards and unnecessary loan applications right before quoting. A cluster of hard inquiries can temporarily knock your CBIS down a notch.</li>
        <li><strong>Days 76–90 (Ask for a Re-Score and Shop Around):</strong> Call your current insurer 21 days before renewal to request a fresh credit-based insurance score pull, and gather competing quotes from two carriers known for lighter credit penalties.</li>
      </ul>

